## 10.2 Database Integrity Rules

- Use UUID primary keys.
- Use proper foreign keys.
- Use transactions.
- Use CHECK constraints.
- Use appropriate unique constraints.
- Use optimistic locking.
- Use row_version.
- Use soft deletion.
- Use audit trails.
- Store timestamps in UTC.
- Use exact decimal types for land area.
- Never use floating-point for legally relevant area values.

A deed number alone must never be treated as globally unique.

Use a configurable duplicate detection policy based on office, year, deed number and applicable register context.

Similar names do not prove identical people.

Same dag number in different mouzas does not identify the same land parcel.

Do not blindly use cascade deletion for legal and historical records.

## 10.3 Database Migrations

Use Flyway for PostgreSQL migrations.

Use Room Migration for local database upgrades.

Every migration must be tested against representative previous database versions.

Never destroy user data during a normal app upgrade.

