# SECTION 12 — SEARCH ENGINE SPECIFICATION

Build a search architecture that can support large cloud datasets without loading all rows into memory.

## 12.1 Version 1 Search

Implement:

1. Exact deed number search
2. Exact year search
3. Exact dag search
4. Exact khatian search
5. Normalized name search
6. Indexed prefix search
7. Combined filters
8. Office-scoped search
9. Mouza-scoped search

## 12.2 Search Normalization

Implement:

- Unicode NFC normalization
- Bengali digit normalization
- English lowercase normalization
- Whitespace normalization
- Punctuation handling
- Original text preservation
- Separate normalized search values
- Curated alias matching

Examples:

১২৫৬ → 1256

১২৫৬ / ২০২৪ → 1256/2024

ABDUL KARIM → abdul karim

আব্দুল  করিম → আব্দুল করিম

Do not make aggressive Bengali character transformations that merge distinct names.

Do not assume মোঃ, মোহাম্মদ, Md. always mean the same individual.

Use aliases as search hints, not identity proof.

## 12.3 Exact Search Algorithm

Use PostgreSQL B-tree indexes and equality comparisons.

Typical complexity of indexed lookup is approximately O(log N).

## 12.4 Prefix Search Algorithm

Use indexed prefix matching on properly normalized columns.

Test the PostgreSQL collation and operator-class configuration.

## 12.5 Local Search

Use Room indexed queries for local records.

Use Room-compatible FTS support only if necessary and tested.

Do not assume Room's FTS4 annotation automatically enables SQLite FTS5.

## 12.6 Future Advanced Search

Prepare an extensible Search Strategy interface for:

- PostgreSQL Full-text Search
- GIN Index
- pg_trgm
- Trigram Similarity
